{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12597","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12597","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12597","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruiqi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Ruiqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jamie","familyName":"Geng","name":"Geng, Jamie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaun","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Shaun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shreyas","familyName":"Srinivasan","name":"Srinivasan, Shreyas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taehyung","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Taehyung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mayuran","familyName":"Saravanapavanantham","name":"Saravanapavanantham, Mayuran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kwang-Hee","familyName":"Lim","name":"Lim, Kwang-Hee","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mike","familyName":"Dillender","name":"Dillender, Mike","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heejae","familyName":"Chung","name":"Chung, Heejae","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thienan","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Thienan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Karen","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Karen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongli","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Yongli","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taegon","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Taegon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Moungi G.","familyName":"Bawendi","name":"Bawendi, Moungi G.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vladimir","familyName":"Bulovic","name":"Bulovic, Vladimir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Morphological and Chemical Changes in Cd-free Colloidal QD-LEDs During Operation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Applied Physics (physics.app-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Materials Science (cond-mat.mtrl-sci)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T02:53:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:18:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Heavy metal-free quantum-dot light-emitting devices (QD-LEDs) have demonstrated remarkable brightness, saturated color, and high efficiencies across a broad spectral range. However, in contrast to organic LEDs (OLEDs), QD-LED operational lifetimes remain limited, with the underlying degradation mechanisms not fully understood. In the present study, we show that InP/ZnSe/ZnS (red-emitting) and ZnTeSe/ZnSe/ZnS (blue-emitting) cadmium-free colloidal QD-LEDs undergo nanoscale morphological changes during operation. Specifically,interparticle coarsening and layer thinning are observed in the electron transport layer (ETL) consisting of ZnMgO nanoparticles (NPs), in the QD emissive layer, and in the organic hole transport layer. This is accompanied by the generation and diffusion of compositional oxygen- and hydrogen-radicals throughout the device, with oxygen accumulating at the electrode/ETL interfance. Moreover, in situ transmission electron microscopy reveals the electron beam exposure, in the presence of hydrogen radicals, accelerates ZnMgO NPs coarsening. To mitigate these degradation pathway, we show that acrylate-based resin-encapsulation treatment stabilize the ETL/QD layers by suppressing the radical formation and halting morphology changes. This approach achieves dramatic stability enhancements, exhibits an 8-fold and 5000-fold lifetime improvement on InP/ZnSe/ZnS and ZnTeSe/ZnSe/ZnS QD-LEDs, respectively. Our findings establish the causal relationships between the morphological degradation, interlayer radical dynamics, and state-of-the-art QD-LEDs instability, providing new insights into a scalable encapsulation treatment that enables efficient and long-lived Cd-free QD-LEDs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"34 pages, 5 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12597","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:44:06Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:44:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:44:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12592","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12592","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12592","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aaron","familyName":"Baughman","name":"Baughman, Aaron","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gozde","familyName":"Akay","name":"Akay, Gozde","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eduardo","familyName":"Morales","name":"Morales, Eduardo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rahul","familyName":"Agarwal","name":"Agarwal, Rahul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Preetika","familyName":"Srivastava","name":"Srivastava, Preetika","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Match Chat: Real Time Generative AI and Generative Computing for Tennis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T02:38:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:17:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present Match Chat, a real-time, agent-driven assistant designed to enhance the tennis fan experience by delivering instant, accurate responses to match-related queries. Match Chat integrates Generative Artificial Intelligence (GenAI) with Generative Computing (GenComp) techniques to synthesize key insights during live tennis singles matches. The system debuted at the 2025 Wimbledon Championships and the 2025 US Open, where it provided about 1 million users with seamless access to streaming and static data through natural language queries. The architecture is grounded in an Agent-Oriented Architecture (AOA) combining rule engines, predictive models, and agents to pre-process and optimize user queries before passing them to GenAI components. The Match Chat system had an answer accuracy of 92.83% with an average response time of 6.25 seconds under loads of up to 120 requests per second (RPS). Over 96.08% of all queries were guided using interactive prompt design, contributing to a user experience that prioritized clarity, responsiveness, and minimal effort. The system was designed to mask architectural complexity, offering a frictionless and intuitive interface that required no onboarding or technical familiarity. Across both Grand Slam deployments, Match Chat maintained 100% uptime and supported nearly 1 million unique users, underscoring the scalability and reliability of the platform. This work introduces key design patterns for real-time, consumer-facing AI systems that emphasize speed, precision, and usability that highlights a practical path for deploying performant agentic systems in dynamic environments."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, 5 Figures, 4 Tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12592","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:43:58Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:43:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:43:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12591","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12591","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12591","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vijay","familyName":"Govindarajan","name":"Govindarajan, Vijay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pratik","familyName":"Patel","name":"Patel, Pratik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sahil","familyName":"Tripathi","name":"Tripathi, Sahil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Md Azizul","familyName":"Hoque","name":"Hoque, Md Azizul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gautam Siddharth","familyName":"Kashyap","name":"Kashyap, Gautam Siddharth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MAGIC-Enhanced Keyword Prompting for Zero-Shot Audio Captioning with CLIP Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T02:36:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:17:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Automated Audio Captioning (AAC) generates captions for audio clips but faces challenges due to limited datasets compared to image captioning. To overcome this, we propose the zero-shot AAC system that leverages pre-trained models, eliminating the need for extensive training. Our approach uses a pre-trained audio CLIP model to extract auditory features and generate a structured prompt, which guides a Large Language Model (LLM) in caption generation. Unlike traditional greedy decoding, our method refines token selection through the audio CLIP model, ensuring alignment with the audio content. Experimental results demonstrate a 35% improvement in NLG mean score (from 4.7 to 7.3) using MAGIC search with the WavCaps model. The performance is heavily influenced by the audio-text matching model and keyword selection, with optimal results achieved using a single keyword prompt, and a 50% performance drop when no keyword list is used."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted in The 26th International Conference on Web Information Systems Engineering (WISE), scheduled for 15-17 December 2025 in Marrakech, Morocco"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12591","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:43:57Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:43:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:43:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12590","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12590","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12590","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shao-Yu","familyName":"Chu","name":"Chu, Shao-Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhe","familyName":"Tian","name":"Tian, Yuhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu-Xiang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yu-Xiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haojian","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Haojian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DPCheatSheet: Using Worked and Erroneous LLM-usage Examples to Scaffold Differential Privacy Implementation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T02:33:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:17:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper explores how programmers without specialized expertise in differential privacy (DP) (i.e., novices) can leverage LLMs to implement DP programs with minimal training. We first conducted a need-finding study with 6 novices and 3 experts to understand how they utilize LLMs in DP implementation. While DP experts can implement correct DP analyses through a few prompts, novices struggle to articulate their requirements in prompts and lack the skills to verify the correctness of the generated code. We then developed DPCheatSheet, an instructional tool that helps novices implement DP using LLMs. DPCheatSheet combines two learning concepts: it annotates an expert's workflow with LLMs as a worked example to bridge the expert mindset to novices, and it presents five common mistakes in LLM-based DP code generation as erroneous examples to support error-driven learning. We demonstrated the effectiveness of DPCheatSheet with an error identification study and an open-ended DP implementation study."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12590","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:43:56Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:43:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:43:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12589","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12589","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12589","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Garima","familyName":"Agrawal","name":"Agrawal, Garima","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Riccardo","familyName":"De Maria","name":"De Maria, Riccardo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kiran","familyName":"Davuluri","name":"Davuluri, Kiran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniele","familyName":"Spera","name":"Spera, Daniele","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Charlie","familyName":"Read","name":"Read, Charlie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cosimo","familyName":"Spera","name":"Spera, Cosimo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jack","familyName":"Garrett","name":"Garrett, Jack","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Don","familyName":"Miller","name":"Miller, Don","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Redefining CX with Agentic AI: Minerva CQ Case Study"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T02:30:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:17:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Despite advances in AI for contact centers, customer experience (CX) continues to suffer from high average handling time (AHT), low first-call resolution, and poor customer satisfaction (CSAT). A key driver is the cognitive load on agents, who must navigate fragmented systems, troubleshoot manually, and frequently place customers on hold. Existing AI-powered agent-assist tools are often reactive driven by static rules, simple prompting, or retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) without deeper contextual reasoning. We introduce Agentic AI goal-driven, autonomous, tool-using systems that proactively support agents in real time. Unlike conventional approaches, Agentic AI identifies customer intent, triggers modular workflows, maintains evolving context, and adapts dynamically to conversation state. This paper presents a case study of Minerva CQ, a real-time Agent Assist product deployed in voice-based customer support. Minerva CQ integrates real-time transcription, intent and sentiment detection, entity recognition, contextual retrieval, dynamic customer profiling, and partial conversational summaries enabling proactive workflows and continuous context-building. Deployed in live production, Minerva CQ acts as an AI co-pilot, delivering measurable improvements in agent efficiency and customer experience across multiple deployments."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12589","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:43:54Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:43:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:43:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12569","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12569","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12569","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Qi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuliang","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Shuliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinjia","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Jinjia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Adaptive Sampling Scheduler"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T01:51:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:15:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Consistent distillation methods have evolved into effective techniques that significantly accelerate the sampling process of diffusion models. Although existing methods have achieved remarkable results, the selection of target timesteps during distillation mainly relies on deterministic or stochastic strategies, which often require sampling schedulers to be designed specifically for different distillation processes. Moreover, this pattern severely limits flexibility, thereby restricting the full sampling potential of diffusion models in practical applications. To overcome these limitations, this paper proposes an adaptive sampling scheduler that is applicable to various consistency distillation frameworks. The scheduler introduces three innovative strategies: (i) dynamic target timestep selection, which adapts to different consistency distillation frameworks by selecting timesteps based on their computed importance; (ii) Optimized alternating sampling along the solution trajectory by guiding forward denoising and backward noise addition based on the proposed time step importance, enabling more effective exploration of the solution space to enhance generation performance; and (iii) Utilization of smoothing clipping and color balancing techniques to achieve stable and high-quality generation results at high guidance scales, thereby expanding the applicability of consistency distillation models in complex generation scenarios. We validated the effectiveness and flexibility of the adaptive sampling scheduler across various consistency distillation methods through comprehensive experimental evaluations. Experimental results consistently demonstrated significant improvements in generative performance, highlighting the strong adaptability achieved by our method."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages, 10 figures,2 Tables, 18 Equations"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12569","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:43:27Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:43:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:43:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12546","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12546","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12546","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yingxin","familyName":"Lai","name":"Lai, Yingxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zitong","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Zitong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linlin","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Linlin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yong","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Yong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaochun","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Xiaochun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Agent4FaceForgery: Multi-Agent LLM Framework for Realistic Face Forgery Detection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T01:05:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:13:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Face forgery detection faces a critical challenge: a persistent gap between offline benchmarks and real-world efficacy,which we attribute to the ecological invalidity of training data.This work introduces Agent4FaceForgery to address two fundamental problems: (1) how to capture the diverse intents and iterative processes of human forgery creation, and (2) how to model the complex, often adversarial, text-image interactions that accompany forgeries in social media. To solve this,we propose a multi-agent framework where LLM-poweredagents, equipped with profile and memory modules, simulate the forgery creation process. Crucially, these agents interact in a simulated social environment to generate samples labeled for nuanced text-image consistency, moving beyond simple binary classification. An Adaptive Rejection Sampling (ARS) mechanism ensures data quality and diversity. Extensive experiments validate that the data generated by our simulationdriven approach brings significant performance gains to detectors of multiple architectures, fully demonstrating the effectiveness and value of our framework."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12546","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:42:54Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:42:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:42:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12531","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12531","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12531","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Scott","familyName":"Jones","name":"Jones, Scott","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liyou","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Liyou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sebastian W.","familyName":"Pattinson","name":"Pattinson, Sebastian W.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Pre-trained Visual Representations Generalize Where it Matters in Model-Based Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.6; I.2.9; I.4.8; F.2.2","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"68T07, 68T40 (Primary) 93C85, 62L20 (Secondary)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T00:13:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:12:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In visuomotor policy learning, the control policy for the robotic agent is derived directly from visual inputs. The typical approach, where a policy and vision encoder are trained jointly from scratch, generalizes poorly to novel visual scene changes. Using pre-trained vision models (PVMs) to inform a policy network improves robustness in model-free reinforcement learning (MFRL). Recent developments in Model-based reinforcement learning (MBRL) suggest that MBRL is more sample-efficient than MFRL. However, counterintuitively, existing work has found PVMs to be ineffective in MBRL. Here, we investigate PVM's effectiveness in MBRL, specifically on generalization under visual domain shifts. We show that, in scenarios with severe shifts, PVMs perform much better than a baseline model trained from scratch. We further investigate the effects of varying levels of fine-tuning of PVMs. Our results show that partial fine-tuning can maintain the highest average task performance under the most extreme distribution shifts. Our results demonstrate that PVMs are highly successful in promoting robustness in visual policy learning, providing compelling evidence for their wider adoption in model-based robotic learning applications."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12531","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:42:33Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:42:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:42:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12528","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12528","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12528","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keita","familyName":"Funayama","name":"Funayama, Keita","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuki","familyName":"Akura","name":"Akura, Yuki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hiroya","familyName":"Tanaka","name":"Tanaka, Hiroya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Hirotani","name":"Hirotani, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Topological Phononic Crystal on the Scale of Quasi-Ballistic Phonon Transport"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Mesoscale and Nanoscale Physics (cond-mat.mes-hall)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Applied Physics (physics.app-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T00:08:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:12:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Phonon engineering technology has opened up the functional thermal management of semiconductor-based classical and quantum electronics at the micro- and nanoscales. However, challenges have remained in designing accurate thermal characteristics based on quasi-ballistic phonon transport. The quasi-ballistic thermal transport arises from the combination of wave-like and diffusive phonon behaviors unlike pure diffusion. The topological nature has been known to be compatible with both wave and diffusive phenomena. Therefore, topological phononic crystals have great potential for the development of controllable and designable thermal transport based on quasi-ballistic phonons. In this study, we experimentally investigated the thermal behavior at the scale of quasi-ballistic phonon transport using a 1D Su-Schrieffer-Heeger model-based topological phononic crystal. Quasi-ballistic phonon transport was observed through change in thermal conductivity depending on the structural parameters of topological systems using micro-thermoreflectance. Furthermore, using topological interface states, the experimentally observed thermal behaviors were found to agree well with the theoretically expected those. Accordingly, the topological nature is an effective approach for thermal management in micro- and nanoscale systems with quasi-ballistic phonon transport. Our results pave the way for a unified control scheme for wave and diffusion phenomena, such as quasi-ballistic phonons."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12528","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:42:29Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:42:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:42:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12522","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12522","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12522","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Devin","familyName":"Hunter","name":"Hunter, Devin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chinwendu","familyName":"Enyioha","name":"Enyioha, Chinwendu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Hybrid State Estimation of Uncertain Nonlinear Dynamics Using Neural Processes"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T23:56:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:12:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Various neural network architectures are used in many of the state-of-the-art approaches for real-time nonlinear state estimation in dynamical systems. With the ever-increasing incorporation of these data-driven models into the estimation domain, models with reliable margins of error are required -- especially for safety-critical applications. This paper discusses a novel hybrid, data-driven state estimation approach based on the physics-informed attentive neural process (PI-AttNP), a model-informed extension of the attentive neural process (AttNP). We augment this estimation approach with the regression-based split conformal prediction (CP) framework to obtain quantified model uncertainty with probabilistic guarantees. After presenting the algorithm in a generic form, we validate its performance in the task of grey-box state estimation of a simulated under-actuated six-degree-of-freedom quadrotor with multimodal Gaussian sensor noise and several external perturbations typical to quadrotors. Further, we compare outcomes with state-of-the-art data-driven methods, which provide significant evidence of the physics-informed neural process as a viable novel approach for model-driven estimation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"32 pages (single column) - 6 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12522","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:42:19Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:42:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:42:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12521","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12521","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12521","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifan","familyName":"Lan","name":"Lan, Yifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuanpu","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Yuanpu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weitong","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Weitong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lu","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Lu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinghui","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Jinghui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Phi: Preference Hijacking in Multi-modal Large Language Models at Inference Time"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T23:55:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:12:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recently, Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) have gained significant attention across various domains. However, their widespread adoption has also raised serious safety concerns. In this paper, we uncover a new safety risk of MLLMs: the output preference of MLLMs can be arbitrarily manipulated by carefully optimized images. Such attacks often generate contextually relevant yet biased responses that are neither overtly harmful nor unethical, making them difficult to detect. Specifically, we introduce a novel method, Preference Hijacking (Phi), for manipulating the MLLM response preferences using a preference hijacked image. Our method works at inference time and requires no model modifications. Additionally, we introduce a universal hijacking perturbation -- a transferable component that can be embedded into different images to hijack MLLM responses toward any attacker-specified preferences. Experimental results across various tasks demonstrate the effectiveness of our approach. The code for Phi is accessible at https://github.com/Yifan-Lan/Phi."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12521","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:42:18Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:42:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:42:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12519","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12519","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12519","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ross","familyName":"Koval","name":"Koval, Ross","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicholas","familyName":"Andrews","name":"Andrews, Nicholas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xifeng","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Xifeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Context-Aware Language Models for Forecasting Market Impact from Sequences of Financial News"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computational Engineering, Finance, and Science (cs.CE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computational Finance (q-fin.CP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7; J.4","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T23:51:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:12:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Financial news plays a critical role in the information diffusion process in financial markets and is a known driver of stock prices. However, the information in each news article is not necessarily self-contained, often requiring a broader understanding of the historical news coverage for accurate interpretation. Further, identifying and incorporating the most relevant contextual information presents significant challenges. In this work, we explore the value of historical context in the ability of large language models to understand the market impact of financial news. We find that historical context provides a consistent and significant improvement in performance across methods and time horizons. To this end, we propose an efficient and effective contextualization method that uses a large LM to process the main article, while a small LM encodes the historical context into concise summary embeddings that are then aligned with the large model's representation space. We explore the behavior of the model through multiple qualitative and quantitative interpretability tests and reveal insights into the value of contextualization. Finally, we demonstrate that the value of historical context in model predictions has real-world applications, translating to substantial improvements in simulated investment performance."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Preprint"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12519","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:42:15Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:42:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:42:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12514","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12514","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12514","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chiara","familyName":"Bonfanti","name":"Bonfanti, Chiara","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michele","familyName":"Colombino","name":"Colombino, Michele","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giulia","familyName":"Coucourde","name":"Coucourde, Giulia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Faeze","familyName":"Memari","name":"Memari, Faeze","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stefano","familyName":"Pinardi","name":"Pinardi, Stefano","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rosa","familyName":"Meo","name":"Meo, Rosa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A comparison of pipelines for the translation of a low resource language based on transformers"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computational Engineering, Finance, and Science (cs.CE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T23:36:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:11:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This work compares three pipelines for training transformer-based neural networks to produce machine translators for Bambara, a Mandè language spoken in Africa by about 14,188,850 people. The first pipeline trains a simple transformer to translate sentences from French into Bambara. The second fine-tunes LLaMA3 (3B-8B) instructor models using decoder-only architectures for French-to-Bambara translation. Models from the first two pipelines were trained with different hyperparameter combinations to improve BLEU and chrF scores, evaluated on both test sentences and official Bambara benchmarks. The third pipeline uses language distillation with a student-teacher dual neural network to integrate Bambara into a pre-trained LaBSE model, which provides language-agnostic embeddings. A BERT extension is then applied to LaBSE to generate translations. All pipelines were tested on Dokotoro (medical) and Bayelemagaba (mixed domains). Results show that the first pipeline, although simpler, achieves the best translation accuracy (10% BLEU, 21% chrF on Bayelemagaba), consistent with low-resource translation results. On the Yiri dataset, created for this work, it achieves 33.81% BLEU and 41% chrF. Instructor-based models perform better on single datasets than on aggregated collections, suggesting they capture dataset-specific patterns more effectively."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 pages, 4 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12514","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:42:09Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:42:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:42:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12507","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12507","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12507","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anna","familyName":"Deichler","name":"Deichler, Anna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siyang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Siyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simon","familyName":"Alexanderson","name":"Alexanderson, Simon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonas","familyName":"Beskow","name":"Beskow, Jonas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Learning to Generate Pointing Gestures in Situated Embodied Conversational Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.9; I.2.6","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"68T07, 68T40","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T23:15:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:11:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.3389/frobt.2023.1110534","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"One of the main goals of robotics and intelligent agent research is to enable natural communication with humans in physically situated settings. While recent work has focused on verbal modes such as language and speech, non-verbal communication is crucial for flexible interaction. We present a framework for generating pointing gestures in embodied agents by combining imitation and reinforcement learning. Using a small motion capture dataset, our method learns a motor control policy that produces physically valid, naturalistic gestures with high referential accuracy. We evaluate the approach against supervised learning and retrieval baselines in both objective metrics and a virtual reality referential game with human users. Results show that our system achieves higher naturalness and accuracy than state-of-the-art supervised models, highlighting the promise of imitation-RL for communicative gesture generation and its potential application to robots."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"DOI: 10.3389/frobt.2023.1110534. This is the author's LaTeX version"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12507","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-17T01:42:00Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:42:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:42:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12492","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12492","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12492","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Organizational","affiliation":[],"name":"Purushoth","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Organizational","affiliation":[],"name":"Alireza","nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Evaluating Robustness of Vision-Language Models Under Noisy Conditions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T22:31:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:11:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-Language Models (VLMs) have attained exceptional success across multimodal tasks such as image captioning and visual question answering. However, their robustness under noisy conditions remains unfamiliar. In this study, we present a comprehensive evaluation framework to evaluate the performance of several state-of-the-art VLMs under controlled perturbations, including lighting variation, motion blur, and compression artifacts. We used both lexical-based metrics (BLEU, METEOR, ROUGE, CIDEr) and neural-based similarity measures using sentence embeddings to quantify semantic alignment. Our experiments span diverse datasets, revealing key insights: (1) descriptiveness of ground-truth captions significantly influences model performance; (2) larger models like LLaVA excel in semantic understanding but do not universally outperform smaller models; and (3) certain noise types, such as JPEG compression and motion blur, dramatically degrade performance across models. Our findings highlight the nuanced trade-offs between model size, dataset characteristics, and noise resilience, offering a standardized benchmark for future robust multimodal learning."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12492","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:41:39Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:41:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:41:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12488","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12488","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12488","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"H.","familyName":"Sana","name":"Sana, H.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"T.","familyName":"Shenar","name":"Shenar, T.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"J.","familyName":"Bodensteiner","name":"Bodensteiner, J.","affiliation":[